a='content/os/01-processes/proc-ipc.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json');src=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pipe.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/unix.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/signal.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/mq_overview.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pid_namespaces.7.html','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/stop/']
for old,new in [("That's what makes a crash in your browser's tab process harmless to the rest of the browser.","This can contain a tab-process fault, though shared resources, browser dependencies and privileged bugs can still affect other components."),('On Linux the default capacity is **64 KiB** (adjustable with `fcntl(F_SETPIPE_SZ)`).','Linux commonly defaults to 16 pages (64 KiB with 4 KiB pages), but resource limits and configuration can reduce it. Query `F_GETPIPE_SZ`; `F_SETPIPE_SZ` requests a resize subject to limits.'),('An anonymous pipe only connects relatives that inherited it.','Anonymous pipes commonly connect processes that inherit them, but descriptors can also be passed to unrelated processes over Unix sockets.'),('which is what makes them a reliable last resort.','though permissions, init semantics and uninterruptible waits can prevent or delay the expected effect.'),('    sa.sa_handler = on_term;','    sa.sa_handler = on_term;\n    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);'),('This is exactly what a graceful shutdown looks like.','This is a schematic shutdown loop: include `<signal.h>`, define the work and cleanup routines, check sigaction errors and ensure do_work returns or responds to shutdown. A flag alone does not wake an indefinitely blocked operation.'),('in the handler, loop `waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG)` until it returns 0.','reap in a `waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG)` loop while returns are positive; handle -1, including EINTR and ECHILD, as well as 0.'),('## Shared memory: the fastest channel','## Shared memory: avoiding payload copies'),('After setup, communication is just ordinary loads and stores, with no syscalls and no copies.','After setup, payload can be accessed with loads and stores without copying it through a kernel channel. Synchronisation, notification, cache coherence and page faults still have costs.'),('/* both processes run this */','/* creator initialises; peers attach */'),('p[0] = 42;  /* the other sees 42 */','p[0] = 42; /* publish with proper sync */'),('Use C11 atomics, a `pthread_mutex` initialised with `PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED`, or a POSIX semaphore placed in the shared region.','Use a process-shared mutex or semaphore. Atomics require a platform/implementation that supports the chosen atomic representation across processes; non-lock-free atomics may use unsuitable process-local locks.'),('so store offsets, not pointers.','so store validated offsets or another relocatable representation rather than assuming raw pointers are usable.'),('always returns the oldest message of the highest priority.','returns the oldest available message at the highest available priority.'),('Unix sockets are typically noticeably faster than TCP over `127.0.0.1`.','Unix sockets avoid those TCP/IP protocol layers, but their performance relative to loopback TCP requires a workload-specific measurement.'),('**TCP sockets** are the only option here that crosses machines.','**TCP sockets** provide the network-capable choice in this comparison; UDP and other network transports also exist.'),('Pipes, queues and sockets cost at least two syscalls and two copies per message, plus a context switch if the receiver was asleep (see the previous lesson). Shared memory costs nothing per message but makes you build synchronisation and notification yourself.','Conventional read/write channel use often copies bytes into and out of kernel buffers. Batching and zero-copy APIs change the per-message costs, and a wake-up does not imply a fixed switch count. Shared memory avoids those payload copies but still pays synchronisation, coherence and notification costs.'),('Pipes are one-way, bounded byte streams (64 KiB by default on Linux);','Pipes are one-way, bounded byte streams (query the actual Linux capacity);'),('Shared memory is the fastest channel because it avoids per-message syscalls and copies, but you must supply synchronisation and notification yourself.','Shared memory can avoid kernel-mediated payload copies, but requires synchronisation and notification; fastest depends on the workload.'),('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content gap: IPC performance\n> No reproducible IPC benchmark is supplied. Universal fastest-channel rankings and fixed copy/syscall counts are omitted. Code fragments illustrate mechanisms and need headers, error handling, ownership and synchronisation before production use.\n\n## Key takeaways')]:edit(a,old,new,'Correct variable pipe capacity, shared-memory synchronisation and IPC cost/notification claims.',src)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q2','explanation','Applications cannot install handlers, block or ignore these signals. Permission checks, init exceptions and uninterruptible waits mean this is not a promise that every kill or stop request takes immediate effect.','Uncatchable does not imply universally immediate termination.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q2','workedExample','SIGKILL and SIGSTOP cannot be caught, blocked or ignored by normal application signal handling. They provide forced termination or stopping without application cleanup, but a caller still needs permission and kernel/init/wait rules affect delivery and timing.','Remove unconditional always-kill assertion.',src)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q3','workedExample','Conventional pipe/socket read-write transfers normally copy payload through kernel buffers. Shared mappings let sender and receiver access the same storage, with an explicit ownership/publication protocol. Synchronisation or notification may make syscalls, and page faults or zero-copy alternatives change costs; the benefit here is avoiding the normal intermediate payload copies.','No universal per-message syscall or copy count.',src)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q6','explanation','Bash status alone is ambiguous because a program can explicitly return 141. In this standard yes/head pipeline with default signal handling, the broken pipe explains SIGPIPE termination.','Status above128 does not uniquely imply signal.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q7','prompt','A blocking Linux pipe is confirmed with F_GETPIPE_SZ to have 65,536-byte capacity and starts empty. A reader keeps its end open but never reads. A writer repeatedly writes 4,096 bytes successfully. How many writes fill it before the next blocks?','Specify actual capacity and open reader rather than universal default.',src)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q7','explanation','One write occupies only 4,096 bytes of the explicitly supplied 65,536-byte capacity.','Keep explanation consistent with capacity assumption.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q8','workedExample','Use the documented async-signal-safe list; being a library wrapper or syscall alone is not the criterion. write and _exit are listed, while printf and malloc are not. A volatile sig_atomic_t flag supports communication with the interrupted execution, but is not general thread synchronisation. Preserve errno if the handler changes it. signalfd is an alternative when the selected signals are blocked in the appropriate threads.','Async-signal safety is a specified contract, not inferred from syscall status.',src)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q10','text','A\'s writes are not interleaved with other writers\' bytes, though reads can split them; B\'s writes may be interleaved','Pipe write atomicity does not imply one read per record.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q12','explanation','Shared memory can avoid payload copies, but later page faults, cache coherence and synchronisation still matter. The claim that it is necessarily slower is unsupported.','Pages do not necessarily remain resident after first fault.',src,option=4)
qe(b,'proc-ipc-q12','workedExample','Unsynchronised increments can lose updates. Use a process-shared mutex/semaphore, or atomics whose implementation supports cross-process use. A named shared-memory object persists until unlinked (or its backing environment is removed); mappings may have different virtual addresses, so use validated offsets. Shared memory can avoid kernel payload copies, but it does not guarantee zero later faults or universally better performance.','Qualify interprocess atomics and repeated page-fault/performance assertions.',src)
save([a,b])
